1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
Tuesday, ten in the morning.

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:06,096
A buyer writes to Malika: my payment failed, here is my card.

3
00:00:06,146 --> 00:00:08,520
The message holds all sixteen digits.

4
00:00:08,570 --> 00:00:13,368
Malika forwards it to Bekzod, the developer who connects her shop to the bank.

5
00:00:13,418 --> 00:00:18,408
Bekzod wants to help, so he pastes the whole number into a support ticket to the bank.

6
00:00:18,458 --> 00:00:24,432
In Lesson 2, that number was all the buyer's bank needed to say yes, so whoever holds it can pay with it.

7
00:00:24,482 --> 00:00:29,616
This lesson gives you the words that let a system talk about a card without ever pasting its number.

8
00:00:29,666 --> 00:00:31,704
The number now lives in the ticket.

9
00:00:31,754 --> 00:00:37,080
It lives in the ticket's backups, in its search, and with everyone who can read tickets.

10
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:40,920
One paste copied a card number into places never built to guard it.

11
00:00:40,970 --> 00:00:45,048
Dilnoza, in the bank's operations team, opens the ticket.

12
00:00:45,098 --> 00:00:50,112
She replies in one line: never send this again, the last four digits are enough.

13
00:00:50,162 --> 00:00:55,488
She talks the way your own bank does on the phone: your card ending seven eight nine zero.

14
00:00:55,538 --> 00:00:58,200
A bank never reads out the whole number.

15
00:00:58,250 --> 00:01:00,888
And it never asks for the three digits on the back.

16
00:01:00,938 --> 00:01:05,112
I think the most dangerous place for a card number is not a hacked server.

17
00:01:05,162 --> 00:01:07,440
It is a helpful person with a text box.

18
00:01:07,490 --> 00:01:09,312
Here are the words.

19
00:01:09,362 --> 00:01:13,632
The long number on the front of the card is the PAN, the card's own number.

20
00:01:13,682 --> 00:01:16,392
It is the one piece a thief needs most.

21
00:01:16,442 --> 00:01:22,728
In this system it lives in one place only: the card zone, the locked room from Lesson 1.

22
00:01:22,778 --> 00:01:25,632
The three digits on the back are the card's security code.

23
00:01:25,682 --> 00:01:30,384
The card industry's rules say nobody may keep it once a payment is approved.

24
00:01:30,434 --> 00:01:33,888
This system goes further: it never even asks for it.

25
00:01:33,938 --> 00:01:37,872
When screens and reports show a card, they show the masked number.

26
00:01:37,922 --> 00:01:42,120
That is the first six digits and the last four, with stars between.

27
00:01:42,170 --> 00:01:45,120
The first six only tell which bank gave out the card.

28
00:01:45,170 --> 00:01:48,096
The last four let a person recognise their own card.

29
00:01:48,146 --> 00:01:52,272
Nothing in between is shown, so the masked number cannot be used to pay.

30
00:01:52,322 --> 00:01:57,576
And when another system needs to point at a card, to charge it later, it keeps a card token.

31
00:01:57,626 --> 00:02:01,272
A card token is a random name the card zone gives the card.

32
00:02:01,322 --> 00:02:03,144
Nothing in it comes from the number.

33
00:02:03,194 --> 00:02:05,808
Make two tokens, and they share nothing.

34
00:02:05,858 --> 00:02:09,144
So a stolen token tells a thief nothing about the card.

35
00:02:09,194 --> 00:02:11,568
Three words, three rules.

36
00:02:11,618 --> 00:02:13,944
The PAN lives only in the card zone.

37
00:02:13,994 --> 00:02:15,816
The masked number may be shown.

38
00:02:15,866 --> 00:02:17,688
The token may be kept.

39
00:02:17,738 --> 00:02:20,040
And the code on the back is kept by no one.

40
00:02:20,090 --> 00:02:23,520
Malika's buyer met those rules on the bank's payment page.

41
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:25,968
Under "Card details" sits the card form.

42
00:02:26,018 --> 00:02:28,272
It asks for the number and the date on the card.

43
00:02:28,322 --> 00:02:30,432
There is no box for the code on the back.

44
00:02:30,482 --> 00:02:33,000
The buyer types the card and continues.

45
00:02:33,050 --> 00:02:38,904
The page now shows one short line: the masked number, the buyer's bank and its card network.

46
00:02:38,954 --> 00:02:42,984
Dilnoza opens the bank admin portal, on its Payments page.

47
00:02:43,034 --> 00:02:44,856
Each payment has a Card column.

48
00:02:44,906 --> 00:02:50,184
Every row shows a masked number: six digits, stars, four digits.

49
00:02:50,234 --> 00:02:52,992
Nowhere in the portal is there a full card number.

50
00:02:53,042 --> 00:02:55,608
So Dilnoza never needed the PAN.

51
00:02:55,658 --> 00:02:58,944
The masked number and the amount are enough to find the payment.

52
00:02:58,994 --> 00:03:03,360
A leaked mask or a leaked token gives a thief nothing to spend.

53
00:03:03,410 --> 00:03:08,928
A leaked PAN lets a thief spend the buyer's money, and the shop and the bank pay for it.

54
00:03:08,978 --> 00:03:14,472
So the PAN stays in one room, and everything else gets a smaller, safer word.

55
00:03:14,522 --> 00:03:16,752
Bekzod deletes the number from the ticket.

56
00:03:16,802 --> 00:03:18,600
The backups keep it anyway.

57
00:03:18,650 --> 00:03:21,384
That is why the rule is never to paste it at all.

58
00:03:21,434 --> 00:03:23,832
Next: where a card number may live.
